Declare a residency, so the battery can measure a client instead of a server

Design and a declaration. **Nothing is built and nothing has been plugged in.**

## The premise that changed

`anscode.ts` opens with a constraint it calls absolute, and it was right to:

> `@minecraft/server-net` says so in its own description: *"This module can only be used on
> Bedrock Dedicated Server."* A client add-on has no HTTP, no socket, no egress of any kind.
> Nothing a person plays on can report anywhere. So on a device with no terminal beside it —
> **an iPad**, a console — the only channel out of the game is the screen.

That is why the answer code exists, why it is Crockford base32, why `redeem` is as paranoid as it
is, and why every `client` row in this ledger was typed in by a person reading 25 characters off
a screen.

**The client's own `/connect` is an egress the add-on API does not have.** It is not `server-net`
and it gives scripts nothing. It is the *client* dialling out to a WebSocket server that may
subscribe to game events and issue commands as the player, enabled by a Creator setting on the
device. So the sentence stays true — an add-on still cannot report anywhere — and stops being the
end of the story, because the game around the add-on now can.

`Platform` has been `bds | client | imported` since the first commit. This is the first thing
that could make the middle value automatic.

## What is in here

- **`residency.yml`** — the claim, in the vocabulary `station-node/.library-engine/RESIDENCY.md`
  already reads. Four labels (`ledger` public and durable, everything else private and transient)
  and two services: a `gateway` listener, and an orderer carrying a `kind:` no host implements.
- **`docs/HARNESS.md`** — what the channel actually is, where each gate goes, and what it is not
  allowed to settle.

## The three gates, and why each one moved

The shape is Stagecraft's relay, deliberately — a human authorising one device to be piloted by
another is the same act. What differs is where the human stands.

**The pairing code changes job, so it changes expiry.** Stagecraft's exists so somebody already
trusted can make a way in for somebody else at another desk. Here the operator and the consenting
human are one person at one station, so there is no consent to transfer; the code only binds a
socket to a pending grant. A consent-transfer code should live long enough to walk somewhere. A
session-binding code should be dead in under a minute.

**The gateway binds loopback, and that is a decision rather than a default.** `/connect` goes over
the network stack — so if the gateway binds a LAN address, any Minecraft on the house wifi can
redeem the code and the physical guarantee the cable seemed to buy was never spent. Binding
loopback and reaching the device through a usbmuxd forward means reachability *is* proof of
attachment, and unplugging is revocation with no policy involved.

**App focus is the liveness gate, and it is mostly self-enforcing.** iOS suspends backgrounded
apps, so Minecraft losing focus kills the socket by itself. Closed, backgrounded and unplugged
reduce to one observable — so don't implement focus as a check; treat socket liveness as the grant
and read focus over USB only to produce a *reason*, because "Minecraft went to background" beats
"socket closed".

## The one unverified thing the whole shape rests on

**Can a Bedrock client on iOS `/connect` to a host reachable only over USB?** usbmuxd's
well-trodden direction is host→device; this wants the reverse. Nobody has plugged the device in.

It is written up as a capability and flagged `!! UNVERIFIED !!` in `residency.yml` rather than
assumed — which is this repository's own discipline applied to its own harness. If it comes back
NO the fallback is in `HARNESS.md`: bind an address, let the code carry the weight, and demote the
cable from transport to witness.

## The rule I most want reviewed

> **A pilot may not reclassify a question by existing.** A row the catalog marks `observed` stays
> eyes-only until a person reclassifies it, one question at a time, with an argument about why its
> outcomes are now differential. Piloted rows carry their own `method`.

A USB device can be screen-recorded, so this could produce a picture — and reading that as
"rendering is measurable now" is the trap. DESIGN §5 says `LOOK` is not a result and cannot be made
into one; §6's cautionary tale is a rig whose two candidate readings produced a **pixel-identical
picture** and cost two sessions. A capture would not have helped there; a differential reading did.

This is the discipline most likely to be eroded quietly by an exciting new capability, so it is
written down before the capability is built rather than after it is abused.

## The hazard the donated hardware brings

The iPad has a broken screen that **fires phantom touch events from one corner**. Two measurement
problems, not annoyances: grants will lapse spuriously, so a lapsed grant must be retryable and
unremarkable; and the piloted client has a second, uninvited pilot, so any reading that depends on
the player not having moved is contaminated. Both are written up.

## Petitioned, not built around

Two items filed at `tiliv/station-node#60`, because both extend somebody else's vocabulary:

- **a fourth `kind:`** — a job enqueued and blocked on hardware that may never arrive. Filed mostly
  as evidence for `a-third-kind-and-the-values-a-host-must-bind.md`, which asked whether kinds are
  a library concern or a cross-engine one; this is a fourth engine from a different group hitting
  the same gaps a day later.
- **a third grant mode** — sibling to the already-filed keeperless one. Vended and chronicled, but
  with a cable for an attestation source and a continuously-held gesture for consent.

`residency.yml` carries the undeliverable declaration with a comment saying no host can order from
it, which is what `RESIDENCY.md` instructs for exactly this shape.

## Verified

- `npm test` — 236 pass, 0 fail (unchanged; this PR adds no code)
- `bedshock validate` — 70 capabilities across 7 domains, 28 observations, all consistent
- **Nothing plugged in.** Every claim about USB, `/connect` and focus is design, and the one the
  rest depends on is marked unverified in the file itself.

## Not done, deliberately

- **The transport.** Writing a usbmuxd bridge before anyone can plug the device in would be
  guessing in code instead of in prose.
- **The gateway and the orderer.** Both wait on the transport answer.
- **Whether a profile is a fourth `Platform` value.** `bds | client | imported` does not
  distinguish an iPad from a Windows box, and some of what a client rig is *for* is exactly that
  difference. It is a ledger schema question and it is named, not answered.
